WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.957
SQL is written in one order, but it is useful to think about its logical processing in another order.

00:00:05.957 --> 00:00:10.612
For a simple query, PostgreSQL first identifies the source in FROM, then applies WHERE to filter rows.

00:00:10.612 --> 00:00:12.827
SELECT defines the result expressions.

00:00:12.827 --> 00:00:15.392
DISTINCT can remove repeated result rows.

00:00:15.392 --> 00:00:20.127
ORDER BY sorts the remaining result, and LIMIT keeps the requested number of rows.

00:00:20.127 --> 00:00:22.897
This mental model helps explain why filtering happens before sorting.

00:00:22.897 --> 00:00:29.402
It also helps explain why an alias created in SELECT is not always available in earlier logical stages.

00:00:29.402 --> 00:00:33.667
You do not need to memorize database-engine internals.

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:34.122
Use this as a reasoning model.

00:00:34.122 --> 00:00:38.952
When a query behaves differently from what you expected, ask which stage creates or removes the value you are

00:00:38.952 --> 00:00:46.147
trying to use. Connect this idea to an analyst exploring operational records before building a report.

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:49.642
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:49.642 --> 00:00:56.057
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:56.057 --> 00:00:59.652
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:59.652 --> 00:01:04.380
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
